  "body": "## What changed\n\nA binary STL writes each corner as f32. Corners closer than one f32 step become one corner in the file, so a stock mesh that passed the closed check in f64 could be written with repeated corners and edges not on two facets.\n\n- `stock_export::file_bytes` (new, `crates/app/src/stock_export.rs`) judges the mesh as the file will hold it: it rounds the corners to their written f32 values (`repair::weld_at_f32`). When a facet collapses at those values, or two distinct corners round to one, it welds bit-exact and checks the result with `unpaired_edges`, refusing by name if the written mesh would not be closed. A mesh with nothing to collapse writes byte for byte as before. `write_stl` and the UI's stock export (`crates/ui/src/state.rs`, same line count) use it.\n- In that written-corner weld only, duplicate facets settle by edge parity (`duplicate_sets` with `Duplicates::ByParity`): a set of copies whose edges the rest of the mesh uses an even number of times keeps no copy; otherwise the first copy stays. Keeping one copy of a back-to-back pair left that pair's three edges on one facet each.\n- `repair::weld`, which `RepairModel` runs on a part model, keeps main's rule (`Duplicates::KeepFirst`): the first copy of each duplicate set stays. The first version of this PR used parity there too, and review found that it removed double-sided surfaces of a part model (finding 1). The export's input is a closed surface, so a duplicate set there can only be two faces the rounding laid together.\n\nWhy parity and not the issue's \"k mod 2\" (keep a copy when the count is odd): k mod 2 removes a single stray duplicate on a closed solid (k = 2), which the existing pin `drop_degenerate` keeps (a flipped duplicate on a cube stays as one copy). Parity keeps that case as pinned and removes the back-to-back pair the weld leaves.\n\nThe decision is in `docs/decisions/4182-stl-as-written.md`.\n\n## Pinning tests\n\nSynthetic shapes only.\n- `crates/app/tests/stock_export.rs` `the_exported_file_is_closed_as_written_and_a_clean_mesh_writes_unchanged`: on main `e6849c5ae` with only the test added it fails, facets written left `6`, right `4`. On this branch it passes.\n- `crates/app/tests/stock_export.rs` `two_solids_whose_edges_meet_in_the_file_are_refused`: on head `025f59e36` (no corner-meet check) it fails, the bytes are written. On this branch it is refused by name.\n- `crates/app/tests/stock_export.rs` `a_sliver_that_collapses_to_nothing_as_written_is_refused`: passes on this branch (refused, \"collapses to nothing\").\n- `crates/geometry/tests/repair.rs` `a_back_to_back_pair_left_at_the_written_corners_goes_and_the_mesh_stays_closed`: through `weld_at_f32`, the thin tetrahedron gives (triangles after, dropped) `(0, 4)` and no open edge. With one copy kept, it was `(1, 3)` and open (the reviewer's mutation M1 on `025f59e36`).\n- `crates/geometry/tests/repair.rs` `corners_closer_than_an_f32_step_weld_at_their_written_values`: passes on this branch.\n- `crates/app/tests/repair_model.rs` `a_double_sided_sheet_keeps_its_facets_through_repair` and `a_double_sided_fin_on_a_cube_stays_through_repair` (finding 1): on `025f59e36`'s repair both fail. The sheet is refused (\"would update the model to empty geometry\"), and the fin cube drops 4 facets, where 2 is expected. On this branch both pass.\n\nOn this branch: repair 12 tests, repair_model 7, stock_export 5, all pass.\n\n## Gates\n\nOn head `217de3194` (base upstream/main `1c0152c91`):\n- `cargo xtask gate quick --base upstream/main`: green, 1226 steps, 0 failed, 0 stalled (101.5 min).\n- `cargo clippy -p camcraft-geometry -p camcraft-app --all-targets -- -D warnings`: clean.\n- `cargo xtask gate light --base upstream/main`: green, 6 steps, 0 failed, 0 stalled (34.2 min).\n- No heavy step: this is not a `P1`. No post or toolpath changed, so no example catalog suite.\n\nOn the first head `025f59e36` the quick gate was green (1226 steps, 128.5 min) and the light lane green (6 steps, 65.8 min).\n\nTrial merge with upstream/main `903e420b3`: clean (`git merge-tree`).\n\n## Not exercised\n\n- The heavy lanes.\n- A mesh whose f32 collapse leaves a non-manifold edge that bit-exact welding cannot settle, other than the two solids whose edges meet: `file_bytes` refuses it by name.\n- `RepairModel` on other double-sided shapes: the pins cover a sheet and a fin; `weld` is main's code path for every caller.\n- In our own lane (outside this repository), two cut stock meshes that were closed in f64 showed 3 to 6 edges on 3 or more facets and 4 to 8 zero-area facets as written. Those meshes are not attached; the pins above are the evidence here.\n\nEffort: high (stock export of simulated stock).\nChangelog: Stock export: a machined stock mesh is written as a closed solid even where its corners are closer than the STL's float precision; corners that merge in the file are welded and checked before writing.\n\nBranch: from upstream/main `e6849c5ae`, two commits; the diff is 7 files.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nFixes #4182\n",
